ve u.s hospitals  and that leads to worse health outcomes  for people of color this may not be a  surprise to people but there are the  numbers to back it up what can we make  out of this  well number one if you're a leader of  one of these top institutions you should  be embarrassed you should be ashamed  because what u.s news and world report  is telling us is these aren't the top  health care institutions for americans  they're the top-end health care  institutions for white americans four  out of five hospitals in this country  are serving lower than their demographic  share of black and brown people  they're not having equitable access to  cancer surgeries to heart surgeries to  orthopedic surgery they could get them  back out walking and living life again  and uh kudos to u.s news and world  report for calling it out but next year  they need to incorporate it into the  overall rankings and lift up places like  oklahoma university health places like  eskenazi health where i work in  indianapolis who actually serve  populations that represent  that represent the surrounding  demographics  go ahead well i was going to say dr  adams one way to close this gap is is on  the hospital side but also on the  patient side another finding from the  study was that even black residents who  have insurance are less likely to get  preventative care and then show up with  a big issue how do you address that  well we know that there is a trust gap  and part of that trust gap comes from  your workforce we need to make sure at  these big institutions we're recruiting  doctors and nurses and other staff that  look like the surrounding community they  need to step out and get into the  community i've been to some of these top  institutions hopkins cleveland clinic  ucla and i've challenged them i said how  come you'll send people around the world  to give care but the people on your  campus are scared to go two blocks off  of the hospital campus and you aren't  investing in the surrounding communities  we need to build that trust people need  to know that you care before they care  what you know and these institutions  need to show those surrounding  communities they care so that they'll  feel comfortable coming there you know  dr adams a lot of people have talked  about the distrust that some minorities  feel when it comes to the healthcare  system but when you read studies that  show typically people of color receive  care at lower quality hospitals um and  that contributes to some of the outcomes  that we're talking about that tony  mentioned maybe the mistrust is is not  all that surprising if the care is lower  quality is the study fine right exactly  that was my question dr adams i mean is  there is there at least a correlation  between that  there is a correlation but here's what  you need to understand  a lot of times these big institutions  they get big donations they take care of  more people who have commercial paying  because they're put on these u.s news  and world report top rankings and the  hospitals out there that are taking care  of the black and brown people they're  getting less funding they're less able  to recruit staff they have worse staff  to patient ratios and it ends up being a  cycle a self-perpetuating cycle so we  need to invest in these hospitals that  are taking care of black and brown  people and we need to challenge these  hospitals that aren't to do better  dr jerome adams thank you very very much  as always we appreciate it  thank you get vaccinated vaccines.gov  good advice thank you doctor
 

